{{- /* The document: head, <body> with the signal declarations, and #app holding the page
itself. Server-rendered from current session state -- no client-side bootstrap, no
hydration, so view-source is the state of the quiz and a reload resumes it exactly.

#app is what interactions patch, whole ("fat morph" -- see app.gohtml). Only the toast slot
is patched finer, because a single answer shows several in sequence. */ -}}
<!DOCTYPE html>
{{- /* `data-theme` goes on the ROOT, not on <body>, and that is not a preference: the canvas
and the scrollbars take `color-scheme` from the root and nowhere else, and Pico and Bulma
both document `data-theme` on <html>, so putting it there switches the frameworks' own dark
themes for free.

It is `false` -- not 'auto', not '' -- when the OS should decide: datastar removes an
attribute set to false, and NO attribute is what leaves `color-scheme: light dark` in
charge. The attribute is rendered TWICE over: once statically from the cookie, which is what
makes the first paint right, and once as `data-attr`, which keeps it right after a click.
Without the static half a remembered choice arrives a frame late. */ -}}
<html lang="en"{{ .ThemeAttr }}
      data-attr:data-theme="$_theme && $_theme !== 'auto' ? $_theme : false">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>{{ .Variant.Title }}</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    {{- /* The CSS A/B/C: `$_css` swaps the whole stylesheet with no reload and no server
    involvement. The href is BUILT from the signal rather than a chain of ternaries, which
    makes the file naming the contract: `hand` is `app.css` and anything else is
    `app-<signal>.css`.

    The empty branch is not defensive noise. This element is in <head>, so it is processed
    BEFORE `data-signals` on <body> has declared anything, and an undefined signal reads as
    '' -- which built `/static/app-.css` and fetched a 404 on every single page load before
    the real value arrived a tick later. */ -}}
    <link rel="stylesheet"
          data-attr:href="$_css ? ($_css === 'hand' ? '{{ .Prefix }}/static/app.css' : '{{ .Prefix }}/static/app-' + $_css + '.css') : '{{ .CSSHref }}'"
          href="{{ .CSSHref }}" />
    {{- /* The game-feel layer, loaded AFTER the base sheet: every rule in it is scoped to
    `body.juice`, so it is inert until the Appearance toggle turns it on. One file rather
    than three copies is the whole reason it is separate -- the experiment can be deleted by
    deleting a file and one signal. */ -}}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ .Prefix }}/static/juice.css" />
    {{- /* inline, so a bare deployment does not 404 looking for one */ -}}
    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 16 16'><text y='13' font-size='13'>&#9824;</text></svg>" />
    {{- /* vendored, not the CDN: works offline and pins the version we tested against */ -}}
    <script type="module" src="{{ .Prefix }}/static/datastar.js"></script>
  </head>
  {{- /* `data-init` runs once on load. The drawer starts CLOSED at every width: what is left
  in it is difficulty, the filter, topics, ladder mode, the target, Restart and Appearance,
  every one of which restarts the quiz or is a one-off preference.

  `juice` on the <body>, not inside `#app`: it must survive every morph, and every rule in
  `juice.css` hangs off it. */ -}}
  <body data-signals="{{ .InitialSignals }}" data-attr:data-font="$_font"
        data-class="{juice: $_juice}"
        data-init="$_navOpen = false{{ if eq .TimerMode "stream" }}; @get('{{ .Prefix }}/timer{{ .VariantQuery }}'){{ end }}">
    {{- /* The morph target. Its contents are `app.gohtml`, INCLUDED rather than duplicated:
    this file and the fat patch must render the same markup, and when they were two copies
    they drifted within the hour. */ -}}
    <div id="app">
      {{ template "app.gohtml" . }}
    </div>

    {{- /* --- sound ---

    OUTSIDE `#app`, and that is the whole design. The morph target is rewritten on every
    interaction; an <audio> element inside it would be replaced mid-playback, and the browser
    would re-fetch each file every time. Out here they are loaded once per page and survive
    everything.

    `data-attr:src` rather than a static `src`: with sound off (the default) these elements
    have no source, so a player who never turns it on never requests a WAV. Turning it on
    sets all five sources at once and `preload="auto"` fetches them immediately. `?v=` is the
    build stamp, so a changed synth reaches a browser that has the old file cached.

    The sounds themselves are synthesised in internal/sfx and served from `/sfx/<name>` --
    no binary assets in the repo. There is no volume control, deliberately: setting `.volume`
    needs real JavaScript, and the levels are baked into the samples instead. */ -}}
    {{ range .SfxNames }}
    <audio id="sfx-{{ . }}" preload="auto"
           data-attr:src="$_sound ? '{{ $.Prefix }}/sfx/{{ . }}?v={{ $.BuildStamp }}' : false"></audio>
    {{- end }}
    {{- /* The sink the beats are appended to. Cleared at the start of every answer stream, so
    it holds at most the handful of markers one answer produced. */ -}}
    <div id="sfx" hidden aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </body>
</html>
